Only a volume change from a remote raises volume_changed.

The supervisor behind --onevent owns this device's volume: its master
is what the speakers play at, and it keeps Spotify's volume in step
with that. Two volume_changed events were not a remote's, and both
made the supervisor set its master to something it had not chosen.

A volume sent on the control socket came straight back. Spirc's
set_volume reported it through --onevent, and the supervisor applied
it to its master a second time. By then the master had often moved on,
so a drag played back as the level jumping to an old value and then to
the new one. The socket's `volume` now goes through
Spirc::mirror_volume, which does everything set_volume does (mixer,
cache, the delayed update that tells the remotes) except emit the
event.

And activation reported the device's own level as if a remote had set
it: whatever librespot started with, 50% by default, which would take
the speakers there on every connect. Activation now reports no volume.
The supervisor sends its level on the control socket when the session
starts instead, and the phone shows what the speakers play at.

A volume from a remote still raises the event, and that is the only
kind the supervisor needs to hear.

Applies on top of librespot-control-socket.patch,
librespot-session-loss-reconnect.patch,
librespot-play-from-stopped.patch and
librespot-pause-keeps-position.patch.

